{"_set": "items", "question_id": "mvar_866b4e_4", "model": "claude-opus-5", "problem_id": "mvar_866b4e", "family": "multivariate_regression", "type": "two_choice_loss_compare", "metric": "test_mse", "ratio": 1.327, "correct": "B", "answer": "B", "is_correct": true, "raw_response": "**Calibration from references:**\n\n- Wide networks (width 256) consistently land in the 0.78–0.81 range: ref 3 (depth 2, w256) = 0.783, ref 4 (depth 6, w256) = 0.787, ref 5 (depth 5, w256) = 0.811.\n- Narrow networks do noticeably worse: ref 1 (width 64) = 0.866, ref 2 (width 32, residual) = 0.969. Width is the dominant factor at this low LR/short budget — small widths underfit badly, and residual connections don't rescue them (ref 2 is the worst).\n\n**Option A:** width 16, depth 2, residual — even narrower than the worst reference (width 32). Extrapolating the width trend (256→~0.79, 64→0.87, 32→0.97), width 16 should be ≳1.0.\n\n**Option B:** width 256, depth 5, no residual — essentially the same family as ref 5 (0.811) and ref 4 (0.787), with mixed layer norms and a similar activation mix. Expect ~0.79–0.82.\n\nB is clearly the lower-loss setting.\n\n**B**", "content": "**Calibration from references:**\n\n- Wide networks (width 256) consistently land in the 0.78–0.81 range: ref 3 (depth 2, w256) = 0.783, ref 4 (depth 6, w256) = 0.787, ref 5 (depth 5, w256) = 0.811.\n- Narrow networks do noticeably worse: ref 1 (width 64) = 0.866, ref 2 (width 32, residual) = 0.969. Width is the dominant factor at this low LR/short budget — small widths underfit badly, and residual connections don't rescue them (ref 2 is the worst).\n\n**Option A:** width 16, depth 2, residual — even narrower than the worst reference (width 32). Extrapolating the width trend (256→~0.79, 64→0.87, 32→0.97), width 16 should be ≳1.0.\n\n**Option B:** width 256, depth 5, no residual — essentially the same family as ref 5 (0.811) and ref 4 (0.787), with mixed layer norms and a similar activation mix. Expect ~0.79–0.82.\n\nB is clearly the lower-loss setting.\n\n**B**", "reasoning_content": ""}
